We start by setting up the given equation:
\begin{align*}
\frac{252}{\Theta} &= \underline{3\Theta} + \Theta \\
\frac{252}{\Theta} &= 30 + \Theta
\end{align*}
Now, we can cross-multiply to get:
\begin{align*}
252 &= (\Theta)(30 + \Theta) \\
252 &= 30\Theta + \Theta^2 \\
\Theta^2 + 30\Theta - 252 &= 0
\end{align*}
We can solve this quadratic equation using factoring or the quadratic formula. Factoring gives us:
\begin{align*}
(\Theta + 36)(\Theta - 7) &= 0
\end{align*}
So, the possible values for $\Theta$ are $\boxed{-36}$ or $\boxed{7}$. Since we are looking for a digit, the only valid solution is $\boxed{7}$.The answer is: 7
